.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt REDUCO 1
.Os reduco VERSION
.Sh NAME
.Nm reduco
.Nd bring programs back after their world ended
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar dir
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar dir
.Fl p Ar name
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar dir
.Fl r Ar name
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar dir
.Fl x Ar name
.Nm
.Fl v
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
remembers how a program was started, notices whether it ended on purpose
or was killed together with its surroundings, and starts it again on request.
It does not depend on the host it runs under.
.Pp
Without a mode option,
.Nm
lists the records of this host, one per line, sorted by name, with tab
separated fields: state, name, info, working directory and command.
The state is
.Sq alive
when a guard holds the lock of the record, with the guard's process ID as
info, and
.Sq dead
otherwise, with the recorded cause of death as info, or
.Sq -
if there is none.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl d Ar dir
Use
.Ar dir
as the record store.
.It Fl p Ar name
Print the record
.Ar name
as a
.Xr sh 1
command line that changes to the recorded directory and executes the recorded
command.
.It Fl r Ar name
Revive the dead record
.Ar name
in the foreground.
The lock of the record is taken first, so a record that is alive or being
revived elsewhere is refused and reviving the same record twice at once starts
the program once.
The recorded command runs in the recorded directory, or in
.Ev HOME
with a warning if that directory is gone, with the files of the
.Pa env
directory set in its environment on top of the current one.
.Nm
stays as the parent of the program and guards it.
If the program ends by itself, the record is removed.
If
.Nm
receives
.Dv SIGHUP
or
.Dv SIGTERM
meanwhile, or the record has a
.Pa keep
file, the record is kept, and the signal is written to
.Pa died .
A kept record can be revived again.
.Dv SIGINT
and
.Dv SIGQUIT
are ignored by
.Nm
and not by the program.
.It Fl x Ar name
Delete the record
.Ar name .
A record that is alive is refused.
.It Fl v
Print version information to standard output and exit.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ev REDUCO_DIR
Record store, used when
.Fl d
is not given.
.It Ev HOME
The record store defaults to
.Pa $HOME/.reduco .
.El
.Pp
The program started by
.Fl r
gets these variables:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ev REDUCO
The absolute path of its record.
.It Ev REDUCO_PID
The process ID of the guard.
.El
.Sh FILES
Each record is a directory
.Pa name@host
in the record store, where
.Ar name
consists of letters, digits,
.Sq \&. ,
.Sq _
and
.Sq -
and does not start with
.Sq \&. .
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa argv
The command, each argument terminated by a NUL byte.
.It Pa cwd
The working directory.
.It Pa died
The cause of death, if known.
Removed when the record is revived.
.It Pa env/
Optional.
One file per variable, whose bytes are the value.
.It Pa keep
Optional.
If present, the record is kept when the program ends by itself.
.It Pa lock
Held with
.Xr fcntl 2
by the guard while the program runs.
.El
.Pp
The record store is created with mode 0700 and refused if it is not owned by
the caller or is writable by group or others.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Pp
With
.Fl r ,
the exit status is that of the program.
If the program was killed by a signal,
.Nm
kills itself with the same signal.
The status is 127 if the program was not found and 126 if it could not be
executed or no working directory could be entered.
The record is kept in these cases, with the reason in
.Pa died .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr abduco 1 ,
.Xr dvtm 1
